   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.8",
    "topic_title": "Mechanisms of Transport",
    "stem": "A neuron keeps its cytoplasmic Na⁺ concentration near 15 mM, while the surrounding extracellular fluid contains about 145 mM Na⁺. Na⁺ ions that leak into the cell must be continuously removed to maintain this difference. Which mechanism and justification best explain how the cell removes Na⁺ from its cytoplasm?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "A Na⁺ channel, because charged ions cannot cross the hydrophobic bilayer and must pass through a channel protein.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This picks a mechanism from the molecule type alone and ignores gradient direction. Channels allow only passive flow down the gradient, so an open Na⁺ channel would let Na⁺ rush into the cell, not out. Fix: Compare the concentrations on each side and ask which way passive flow would go before choosing a channel."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Exocytosis, because vesicles can fuse with the plasma membrane and release their contents outside the cell.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This confuses bulk vesicle transport with selective ion transport. Exocytosis secretes large molecules such as proteins in vesicles; it is not the mechanism cells use to selectively move individual ions against their gradients. Fix: Ask whether the substance is a large molecule before choosing endocytosis or exocytosis for it."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "A Na⁺/K⁺ pump, because Na⁺ must move from lower concentration inside to higher concentration outside, which requires energy from ATP hydrolysis.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Exporting Na⁺ moves it from about 15 mM to about 145 mM, against its concentration gradient, so passive transport cannot do it. The Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase uses ATP to pump Na⁺ out and K⁺ in, maintaining the electrochemical gradient and membrane potential."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Facilitated diffusion through a carrier protein, because Na⁺ needs a transport protein and carriers move solutes without energy input.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This assumes any protein-mediated transport can move a solute in either direction. Facilitated diffusion is passive and moves solutes only from high to low concentration, so it cannot move Na⁺ out into the higher-concentration fluid. Fix: Check whether movement is low to high, and if so, identify the energy source that powers it."
     }
    ]
   },
