   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.8",
    "topic_title": "Mechanisms of Transport",
    "stem": "A cell has a higher Na⁺ concentration outside than inside, and its membrane potential also favors Na⁺ movement inward. Nevertheless, the cell continually transports Na⁺ outward to maintain this electrochemical gradient. Which mechanism and justification best explain this outward transport?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "A Na⁺ channel allows outward transport because small, charged ions can pass through channels.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Charge-only trap: A channel provides a pathway for Na⁺, but it does not drive net movement against an electrochemical gradient. Under these conditions, passive Na⁺ movement would be inward. Fix: Compare the requested movement direction with the electrochemical gradient before choosing a channel."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The Na⁺/K⁺ pump transports Na⁺ outward without ATP because using a membrane protein makes transport passive.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Protein-means-passive trap: Using a membrane protein does not make transport passive. The Na⁺/K⁺ pump requires ATP to move ions against their electrochemical gradients. Fix: Check whether the protein merely provides a pathway or uses energy to drive movement against a gradient."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The Na⁺/K⁺ pump uses ATP to move Na⁺ outward against its electrochemical gradient.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Both the concentration difference and the electrical force favor inward Na⁺ movement, so outward transport requires energy. The Na⁺/K⁺ pump uses ATP to drive this active transport, helping maintain ion gradients and membrane potential."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Exocytosis transports Na⁺ outward because charged ions must be enclosed in vesicles to leave a cell.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Vesicle-for-charge trap: Charged ions can cross membranes through transport proteins; their charge does not require vesicular export. Exocytosis releases vesicle contents rather than pumping individual Na⁺ ions across the membrane. Fix: Identify whether the material needs an ion-transport protein or bulk transport in a vesicle."
     }
    ]
   },
